WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.740
$200 million is what simcoe's board wants for three school builds, led by a new Collingwood Collegiate. This is Collingwood Weekly, Episode 10. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. I don't live in Collingwood. After that filing, a new family doctor in wasaga beach, a commercial vehicle blitz in Collingwood, then nutrition money at the public board, and roads in weather ahead. Collingwood today reports the Simcoe County District School Board put that request in front of trustees on November 25.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.860
Chief Executive Dawn Stevens said the Ministry of Education opened the

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.180
Capitol call on November 13 and once the business cases by January 5.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.660
First in line is a replacement for Collingwood Collegiate Institute, then an

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.720
addition at Heronia Centennial Elementary in Elmville, then a new secondary

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.960
school in Bradford, West Quillenbury. Past calls have typically held $500

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.320
million for the whole province, so this package is a large slice of a usual

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.660
pot. Aurelia, Severn, and Ramara trustee Jody Lloyd told the meeting the board is excited

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.480
and also realistic that it may get none of the three. She called it a big ask and said the

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.800
result will depend on how good the competition is. Superintendent Corey Vennispin said in October,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.880
these three were picked because talks with the ministry suggested they could draw funding

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.480
and that the ministry still decides. The province prefers projects that are shovel ready

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.940
on land the board already owns, with a finished design and preferably a design copied from a school

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:30.640
already built. The collegiate plan is two stories and would hold 1,134 students. The board will

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.940
not release that design before the province says yes. There has been interest in sharing the

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:40.840
build with other agencies. The expected bill for that school alone is $83 million. The building

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.620
on her Ontario street went up in the 1950s and is the oldest board high school that has not

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.560
already been approved for a rebuild. An inspection in December 2020 found 57% of its components

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.740
at the end of their life.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.440
Fixing those components is put at $21 million,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.500
and the board calls the building prohibitive to repair.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.200
A site has been selected.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.480
Communications manager Sarah Kekowicz said in October,

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.180
the board would be better positioned

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.200
to say more after the plan goes forward.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.500
The board did not say whether that site is newly bought land

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.400
or the ground the school already sits on.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.220
In Elmvale, the ask is a single story edition,

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.160
renovations and heavy site work.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.100
An architect has that design about three quarters done,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.620
and site plan talks with the township of spring water are still open.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.300
The Bradford High School is also expected to cost $83 million.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.160
The board says it would take pressure off Bradford District High School,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.140
which now has 21 portables.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.080
Ben Nispen is to bring a full report on every approved capital project

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.580
to the February Business Committee.

00:02:39.691 --> 00:02:43.411
Barry Today reports Dr. Michael Sipironi has joined the Wessaga Beach Clinic

00:02:43.411 --> 00:02:48.491
already staffed by Dr. Jacob McGavin, Dr. Yannick Sharet, and Dr. Stephanie Rogers.

00:02:48.491 --> 00:02:52.731
Together, they provide primary care to around 4,900 people.

00:02:53.351 --> 00:02:57.051
The town of Wasaga Beach says the recruitment strategy behind that group has delivered

00:02:57.051 --> 00:02:58.651
four physicians in four years.

00:02:59.331 --> 00:03:03.991
CTV News reports Collingwood officers ran a commercial vehicle blitz and took one truck

00:03:03.991 --> 00:03:04.591
off the road.

00:03:05.131 --> 00:03:06.171
Eight trucks were inspected.

00:03:06.791 --> 00:03:09.811
Truck news reports those inspections produced 14 charges.

00:03:10.471 --> 00:03:15.291
Midland today reports nearly 100 people from across Simco County gathered in Midland as

00:03:15.291 --> 00:03:18.011
work to revive camps in Presca started to take shape.

00:03:18.491 --> 00:03:20.391
The summer camp is 77 years old.

00:03:20.791 --> 00:03:23.111
The frame they are using is one cabin at a time.

00:03:23.611 --> 00:03:27.971
At the council table in Meiford, the Owen Sound Current reports counselors are questioning

00:03:27.971 --> 00:03:34.631
a land purchase of $3,675,000 after the province ruled out highway access to that land.

00:03:35.665 --> 00:03:40.165
Simco County's public board is back asking for student food money, and Collingwood today

00:03:40.165 --> 00:03:41.865
has the November 25th debate.

00:03:41.865 --> 00:03:47.445
Chair Brandi Rafik, Trustee for Agala Tosa Rantio, Clear View, Canadian Forces

00:03:47.445 --> 00:03:51.265
Bay's Board in Anessa, told trustees that a commitment last year from the

00:03:51.265 --> 00:03:55.125
Ministry of Children, Community and Social Services, and from Peterborough

00:03:55.125 --> 00:03:58.605
Child and Family Services is not turning out as the board hoped.

00:03:59.065 --> 00:04:02.985
Trustees approved a new letter from Rafik to both agencies, written for the

00:04:02.985 --> 00:04:07.445
board, saying again that the programs need more dollars. She also sits with the

00:04:07.445 --> 00:04:11.845
local Eat Well to Excel chapter. Before the board pushed, the ministry had not

00:04:11.845 --> 00:04:16.645
raised province-wide student food dollars since 2014, and newly opened schools

00:04:16.645 --> 00:04:20.785
sat on a wait list with none of that funding. Last year the ministry said it would

00:04:20.785 --> 00:04:24.865
pull new schools off that list using money from the federal $1 billion national

00:04:24.865 --> 00:04:28.665
school food program, and it promised nutrition funding for new schools would

00:04:28.665 --> 00:04:33.485
continue. At the latest eat well-to-excel meeting, Rafique said the chapter heard

00:04:33.485 --> 00:04:37.745
the opposite. Schools being built now would get no student nutrition dollars and

00:04:37.745 --> 00:04:41.185
would go on to a new wait list. She said Peterborough child and family

00:04:41.185 --> 00:04:45.885
services was told around this time last year to clear every school off the old waiting list,

00:04:46.105 --> 00:04:50.705
and did. But no instruction for the future came with that order. She called it a hard decision

00:04:50.705 --> 00:04:54.945
for them. Fifteen new schools are coming to Simcoe County within the next few years.

00:04:55.385 --> 00:04:59.485
She said the pot itself has not grown. It has only been spread across more schools.

00:04:59.945 --> 00:05:04.405
Trustee Jody Lloyd pointed to advocacy from the Ontario Public School Board Association

00:05:04.405 --> 00:05:08.685
and to those federal dollars. She said that even though more money has been announced

00:05:08.685 --> 00:05:10.345
and the intentions are well-meaning.

00:05:10.765 --> 00:05:12.305
There are still not sufficient funds,

00:05:12.665 --> 00:05:14.905
and that Simco County has significant challenges.

00:05:15.445 --> 00:05:19.365
Public Health Ontario figures show 26% of Simco County households

00:05:19.365 --> 00:05:22.845
experience food insecurity in 2023-24,

00:05:23.285 --> 00:05:25.645
above the provincial average of 24%.

00:05:25.645 --> 00:05:29.665
The Simco County Green Belt Coalition says 1 in 6 children in youth

00:05:29.665 --> 00:05:31.265
in the county live with food insecurity.

00:05:31.905 --> 00:05:35.685
Earlier this year, trustees heard that about 17,000 of the board's

00:05:35.685 --> 00:05:39.985
27,000 students use the programs across 105 schools.

00:05:40.345 --> 00:05:44.985
Eat well to excel is the nonprofit partnership that has backed volunteer breakfast, lunch

00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:48.665
and snack programs in every Simco County school for more than 20 years.

00:05:49.245 --> 00:05:53.985
Around the table are the Public Board, the Simco-Moscoka District Health Unit, United Way

00:05:53.985 --> 00:05:58.945
Simco-Moscoka, the Simco-Moscoka Catholic District School Board, the Sharing Place Food

00:05:58.945 --> 00:06:02.485
Center, the Karma Project and Peterborough Child and Family Services.

00:06:02.485 --> 00:06:06.365
Provincial-based funding moves through Peterborough Child and Family Services.

00:06:06.745 --> 00:06:14.025
The ministry gave the public board $790,717 in the 2024 to 2025 school year.

00:06:14.485 --> 00:06:21.425
The board estimates ministry and organizational money covers 10 to 15% of what each school actually spends to run the program.

00:06:21.925 --> 00:06:27.005
Fundraising by the school and the community covers the rest, so one school can look nothing like the next.

00:06:27.005 --> 00:06:36.245
New Auro Medante and Springwater Trustees Suelle and Lauren said that split is the part she has an issue with because a strong parent council can raise more money for food.

00:06:36.785 --> 00:06:41.445
Rafiq said communities differ in the relationships, partnerships, and service clubs willing to help.

